
; -----------
; Formulaics
; -----------

:FORM

; A formulaic carries the class a man's rules give it (/reply, /flirtation, /insult ..), so the
; player's line reads as the same line from anyone.

;"Good day to you"                   ( formulaic opening-phrase goodDay @unknown )    .
;"Good day to you, too"              ( formulaic response goodDay @unknown )  .

"Good day"                          ( formulaic [] opening-phrase greeting )              .
"Good day to you"                   ( formulaic [] response greeting )             .

"How do you do"                     ( formulaic [] opening-phrase how_do @unknown )       .
"How do you do <r>"                 ( formulaic [] response how_do @unknown )      .

"How do you do, $name(PNAME)"       ( formulaic [] opening-phrase how_do $name(PNAME) )    .
"How do you do, $name(PNAME) <r>"   ( formulaic [] response how_do $name(PNAME) )  .

;"A moment, if you please; I am engaged in conversation"                        ( formulaic refusal conv busy ) .
"I can't talk right now because $st(ST)"                                        ( formulaic [] refusal $st(ST) ) .
"I can't stop now"                                                              ( formulaic [] refusal busy ) .
"I can't talk right now because I am talking with someone"                     ( formulaic [] refusal in_talk ) .
"I have had my fill of company"                                                 ( formulaic [] refusal weary ) .
"I must go now because $st(ST)"                                                 ( formulaic [] leave-taking excuse $st(ST) ) .
"Leave me be"                                                                   ( formulaic [] rebuff ) .
"It is over between us"                                                        ( formulaic [] breakup ) .

; Letter envelope lines. "Signed, <name>" is the AUTHOR rider, "To <name>" the
; ADDRESSEE rider: the composer (compose_nl_msg) lifts a (/rider ..) form into the
; msg's rider list, never its content, and decode resolves @i / @you from them.
; $name(PNAME) takes a proper name ([n ...]) or a ?var bound to a name-symbol.
; Case-insensitive via the lowercasing preprocess.
"Signed, $name(PNAME)"                                                          ( /author $name(PNAME) ) .
"To $name(PNAME)"                                                               ( /addressee $name(PNAME) ) .

"You look lovely today"                                                        ( formulaic [] compliment ) .
"You are rather charming yourself"                                             ( formulaic [] return-compliment ) .
"You flatter me"                                                               ( formulaic [] response flattered ) .
"Mind your manners"                                                            ( formulaic [] manners-refusal ) .
"It is indeed"                                                                  ( formulaic [] response agree ) .
"Nothing much"                                                                  ( formulaic [] response no-news ) .
; How he knows what he told, asked who told him or how he knows: the man he says it to (just now,
; in this talk, or before), another, or nobody - he saw it.
"Nobody ++, I saw it myself"                                                   ( formulaic [] response saw-it who )
:: realize-only
.
"I saw it myself"                                                               ( formulaic [] response saw-it how )
:: realize-only
.
"You did ++, just now"                                                          ( formulaic [] response told-by-you who just-now )
:: realize-only
.
"You did"                                                                       ( formulaic [] response told-by-you who )
:: realize-only
.
"You told me ++, just now"                                                      ( formulaic [] response told-by-you how just-now )
:: realize-only
.
"You told me"                                                                   ( formulaic [] response told-by-you how )
:: realize-only
.
"$who(NP) did"                                                                  ( formulaic [] response told-by who $who(NP) )
:: realize-only
.
"$who(NP) told me"                                                              ( formulaic [] response told-by how $who(NP) )
:: realize-only
.
; What he knows of it is only what the man he says it to told him.
"Only what you told me ++: that $a(ST)"                                        ( formulaic [] response only-told $a(ST) )
:: realize-only
.
"Only what you told me ++: that $a(ST) ++, and that $b(ST)"                    ( formulaic [] response only-told $a(ST) $b(ST) )
:: realize-only
.
"Only what you told me ++: that $a(ST) ++, that $b(ST) ++, and that $c(ST)"   ( formulaic [] response only-told $a(ST) $b(ST) $c(ST) )
:: realize-only
.
"Only what you told me ++: that $a(ST) ++, that $b(ST) ++, that $c(ST) ++, and that $d(ST)"   ( formulaic [] response only-told $a(ST) $b(ST) $c(ST) $d(ST) )
:: realize-only
.
"How interesting"                                                               ( formulaic [] response receipt ) .
; A statement answered as a courtesy, never a claim: the fact was news to him or he knew it, then
; what it made him feel, or the condolence a courteous man offers for a misfortune he feels nothing
; for.
"I did not know that $fact(ST) ++, and $feeling(ST)"                            ( formulaic [] did-not-know $fact(ST) $feeling(ST) ) .
"I did not know that $fact(ST) ++, and I am sorry to hear it"                   ( formulaic [] did-not-know $fact(ST) condolence ) .
"I did not know that $fact(ST)"                                                 ( formulaic [] did-not-know $fact(ST) ) .
"Yes, I know that $fact(ST) ++, and $feeling(ST)"                               ( formulaic [] already-knew $fact(ST) $feeling(ST) ) .
"Yes, I know that $fact(ST) ++, and I am sorry for it"                          ( formulaic [] already-knew $fact(ST) condolence ) .
"Yes, I know that $fact(ST)"                                                    ( formulaic [] already-knew $fact(ST) ) .

"No, I don't know"                                                              ( formulaic [] no-dont-know ) .
"Yes, it's $answer(NP)"                                                         ( formulaic [] yes-its $answer(NP) ) .
"Yes, this is my $rel(REL) $name(PNAME)"                                        ( formulaic [] yes-this-is $rel(REL) $name(PNAME) ) .

; An introduction: two people made known to each other at once, each told who the other is to him.
; The told message rides inside it, and a hearer adopts it as he would the message alone.
"$first(NP) ++, this is your $rel(REL) $second(NP) ++, and $second(NP) ++, this is your $converse(REL) $first(NP)"
( formulaic [] introduction ( msg [] { $first(NP) /ever $rel(REL) $second(NP) } { $second(NP) /ever $converse(REL) $first(NP) } ) )
:: realize-only
.

"..."                                                                           ( formulaic [] opening-phrase player_talk ) .
"Excuse me, I am a $job(KIND|/isa|job)"                                         ( formulaic [] opening-phrase authority $job(KIND|/isa|job) ) .
"Yes?"                                                                          ( formulaic [] response player_talk ) .
"Yes, $job(KIND|/isa|job) ++?"                                                  ( formulaic [] response authority $job(KIND|/isa|job) ) .

; The player's leave-taking shows the same three dots as his hail; the hail is what "..."
; reads as, so this one is said and never read.
"..."                                                                           ( formulaic [] leave-taking player_bye ) :: realize-only .




; Leavetaking (initiating)
;"Bye"
"Goodbye"
( formulaic [] leave-taking bye ) .

; Leavetaking (response)
;"Bye"
;"Goodbye"
